                <pb facs="0297" n="199" sameAs="frus:frus1900/pg199"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Conger to the
                            Secretary of State.</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Telegram.—Paraphrase.]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Pekin</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1900-08-30"><hi rendition="#italic">August 30,
                        1900</hi></date>.<lb/>(Via Taku, September 4, 1900.)</dateline></opener>
                <p>(Mr. Conger reports that Prince Ching has sent word he has requested edict
                    ordering him to return to Pekin about September 3. Requests most definite
                    instructions possible in view of military occupation, other difficult
                    conditions, and Department’s knowledge of facts <pb facs="0298" n="200"
                        xml:id="pg200"/> unknown in Pekin. Reports are being received daily from the
                    interior of murders of missionaries and converts. An Imperial decree was
                    published July 2, saying that all missionaries of every nationality must at once
                    be driven away to their own countries, so that they may not linger here or make
                    trouble. * * *)</p>
            </div>
        </body>
